concepts.text:
Michigan public assistance runs through the Bridges system, and one application can open several programs at once: the paper MDHHS-1171 Assistance Application or a MI Bridges online application, including one that requests a SER service (ERM 103, verified 10-1-2026).

Medicaid (MA) is health coverage — it is not EBT; eligible people get a mihealth card. Group composition: BEM 211.

FAP (Food Assistance Program) is Michigan's SNAP: monthly food benefits issued on the Michigan Bridge Card. Group composition: BEM 212.

FIP (Family Independence Program) is Michigan's TANF: a monthly cash grant for families with a dependent child. Group composition: BEM 210; grant standards: RFT 210.

SDA (State Disability Assistance) is state cash for disabled or special situations (group composition: BEM 214).

SER (State Emergency Relief) pays one-time crisis help — energy, water and sewer, relocation, home ownership, home repairs, and burial — and it is paid to the vendor (the utility company, landlord, or funeral director) rather than handed to the applicant, consistent with the SER manual structure (ERM 103, 205, 208, 301, 303).

MI Bridges ([newmibridges.michigan.gov](https://newmibridges.michigan.gov)) is the online door: apply, upload verification documents, renew, report changes, and read notices.

Manual map, current as of the BEM 000 table of contents (BPB 2026-024, effective 8-1-2026):
- BEM 205 CDC group composition;
- BEM 210 FIP;
- BEM 211 Medicaid;
- BEM 212 SNAP;
- BEM 214 SDA group composition;
- BEM 220 residence;
- BEM 221 identity;
- BEM 222 concurrent receipt;
- BEM 223 SSNs;
- BEM 225 citizenship and non-citizens.

SER pays one-time crisis help and pays it to the vendor — the utility company, landlord, or funeral director — rather than to the applicant, consistent with the SER manual structure (ERM 103, 205, 208, 301, 303).

THE GROUP RULE (ERM 301, ERB 2026-004, effective 10-1-2026), verbatim:
"All household members are included in the SER group. Income of all household members is budgeted." There is no "purchase and prepare food separately" option inside SER — the household-based group is the controlling structure.

ASSETS (ERM 205, verified 10-1-2026):
- the cash (liquid) asset limit is $15,000 and the non-cash asset limit is $15,000;
- a request at or over the limit is denied for every service except burial;
- the homestead (home plus adjoining land and buildings) is excluded;
- and groups made up solely of FIP, SDA, SSI, Medicaid, or FAP recipients get automatic non-cash eligibility.

ENERGY (ERM 301):
one heat payment and one non-heat electricity payment per fiscal year, not to exceed the SER cap — caps attach to the individual and "follow the individual even if they move from one household to another." An energy crisis is a past-due account or shut-off notice on an energy bill for the household; a fuel tank at or below 25% (or locked despite more); a stated need for deliverable or nontraditional fuel; a prepay account below $100; or an inoperable furnace per a licensed provider.

THE CRISIS CLOCKS:
MDHHS must provide some form of assistance within 48 hours of a crisis application, and within 18 hours for a life-threatening crisis (when disconnection has already happened, restoration is medically necessary, no temporary housing alternative exists, and the Winter Protection Plan does not already protect the home). ERM 301 also states: "There is no income copayment for energy-related services."

APPLYING (ERM 103, verified 10-1-2026):
- file the DHS-1514 SER application ([DHS-1514](https://www.michigan.gov/mdhhs/-/media/Project/Websites/mdhhs/Folder1/Folder101/Application_for_State_Emergency_Relief_DHS_1514_419862_7.pdf)), the MDHHS-1171 with its MDHHS-1171-SER supplement, or through MI Bridges;
- an application may be filed in ANY Michigan county;
- it is registered within one day;
- there is no minimum age (unemancipated minors are referred to children's services without delaying the application);
- and the application accepts an address or a statement of homelessness.

HOUSING AND RELOCATION (ERM 303, verified 10-1-2026):
SER covers moving expenses, security deposits, first month's rent, and rent arrearage; the SER group chooses how to use its authorized relocation amount; and the paperwork trap — a demand for possession or notice to quit is NOT acceptable; a court summons, order, or judgment stating the exact amount is required.

⚠ Verify at MDHHS before relying on:
- the reported roughly-10-business-day decision standard;
- ERM 303's affordability test (reported as new housing costs at or below 75% of net income);
- whether a shut-off notice must name the account holder (ERM 301's crisis definition ties the crisis to "an energy bill for his or her household" — no account-holder requirement appears in the item text, and staff record "Name on account" when calling the provider);
- and the SER Income Need Standards dollar figures (ERM 208 exhibit tables), which this guide deliberately does not publish.

CHANNELS (ERM 103; Michigan Legal Help):
- MI Bridges ([newmibridges.michigan.gov](https://newmibridges.michigan.gov)) — apply, upload documents, track your case, renew, read notices;
- any county MDHHS office;
- mail or fax with the MDHHS-1171;
- SER also accepts the DHS-1514.

DOCUMENTS MDHHS ASKS FOR:
- identification (driver's license, state ID, or passport);
- SSNs for applying members;
- proof of Michigan residency (utility bill, lease, or official mail);
- income for roughly the last 30 days (pay stubs, award letters);
- and rent or mortgage plus utility bills.

FAP:
identity, income, and expense documents less than 30 days old (lease or rent proof, bank statements), birth certificate or SSN card, and proof of rent, utilities, child care, or medical costs where they help — and group-composition factors are verified only if questionable (BEM 212; Michigan Legal Help).

Medicaid:
identity, SSN, residency, citizenship or immigration status, income, and other insurance ([how-to-apply guide](https://medicaidoffice.net/michigan-medicaid/how-to-apply) — verify the exact list at MDHHS).

SER:
proof of the emergency itself (shut-off notice, past-due bill, court eviction paperwork stating the exact amount, a furnace statement), income for the countable period, assets (bank statements), lease and landlord information, and for deliverable fuel the fuel type and tank level — payment is refused if more than 25% remains in the tank.

DECISION CLOCKS:
- FAP expedited by day 7, regular by day 30 (BAM 115);
- SER registered within 1 day, crisis assistance within 48 hours, life-threatening within 18 hours (ERM 103, 301);
- Medicaid about 45 days, or 90 when eligibility turns on a disability determination (42 CFR 435.912).

ADDRESS AND HOUSING RULES (BEM 220, BPB 2026-010, effective 4-1-2026):
- benefits must NOT be denied solely because a person has no permanent dwelling or fixed address;
- a person without housing may use the local office or another agreeable location as the mailing address, and a temporary mailing address must never be recorded as the physical address.

Residence tests differ by program:
FAP/CDC — living in Michigan for any purpose other than vacation, with no permanent-intent test (including people who entered with a job commitment or to seek work, and students home during school breaks); FIP/SDA — adds an intent to remain permanently or indefinitely (rent, property taxes, utilities, or house payments in Michigan count as evidence); Medicaid — living in Michigan except temporary absence.

SER: file in any county; the SER manual is quoted as serving all persons physically present in Michigan — residence in the state is not required (secondary-source quote of ERM — confirm at MDHHS).

Everything in this module was verified against the primary source on 10-1-2026 — the MDHHS manual PDFs on olmweb, eCFR, and MDHHS pages — and every figure carries the effective date from its document header.

MANUAL ITEMS:
- [BEM 210 FIP group composition](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/BP/Public/BEM/210.pdf) (BPB 2026-002, 1-1-2026);
- [BEM 211 Medicaid group composition](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/BP/Public/BEM/211.pdf) (BPB 2026-013, 5-1-2026);
- [BEM 212 SNAP group composition](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/BP/Public/BEM/212.pdf) (BPB 2026-025, 10-1-2026);
- [BEM 220 residence](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/BP/Public/BEM/220.pdf) (BPB 2026-010, 4-1-2026);
- [BEM 554 expenses](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/BP/Public/BEM/554.pdf) (verified 10-1-2026);
- [ERM 103 applications](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/ER/Public/ERM/103.pdf) (verified 10-1-2026);
- [ERM 205 assets](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/ER/Public/ERM/205.pdf) (verified 10-1-2026);
- [ERM 208 budgeting](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/ER/Public/ERM/208.pdf) (ERB 2026-003, 10-1-2026);
- [ERM 301 energy](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/ER/Public/ERM/301.pdf) (ERB 2026-004, 10-1-2026);
- [ERM 303 relocation](https://dhhs.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/ER/Public/ERM/303.pdf) (verified 10-1-2026).

FEDERAL RULES:
cite and read through eCFR — [7 CFR 273.1](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-7/subtitle-B/chapter-II/subchapter-C/part-273/subpart-A/section-273.1) (the SNAP household concept) and [42 CFR 435.603](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/section-435.603) (MAGI households).

TABLES (RFT items live under [RFT tables](https://mdhhs-pres-prod.michigan.gov/olmweb/ex/RF/Public/RFT/250.pdf)):
- RFT 250 FAP income limits and RFT 255 food assistance standards (RFB 2025-006, effective 10-1-2025);
- RFT 210 FIP payment standards (RFB 2025-001, effective 12-2024).

CORRECTION NOTE:
FAP income limits are in RFT 250 — RFT 240 is a different table (Medicaid Monthly Protected Income Levels). Do not cite RFT 240 for FAP.

CURRENCY DISCIPLINE:
MDHHS republishes manual items and RFT tables through the year — before quoting any rule or dollar figure, open the PDF and recheck the header (the BPB, ERB, or RFB letter plus effective date printed in the item). A new-FY RFB effective 10-1-2026 may post at any time and change the dollar figures above.

H.R. 1 BOUNDARY:
the MDHHS Medicaid page ([michigan.gov/mdhhs/assistance-programs/medicaid](https://www.michigan.gov/mdhhs/assistance-programs/medicaid)) carries active notices that federal changes under H.R. 1 affect how Michigan provides Medicaid coverage, plus non-citizen eligibility changes. The notices exist; the substantive changes and implementation timeline are NOT covered by this guide — check that page before relying on any Medicaid rule.

This module is an educational overview, not legal advice; verify at MDHHS.
